{"is_error":false,"duration_api_ms":97445,"num_turns":1,"stop_reason":"end_turn","session_id":"662a404c-74ba-4806-9660-5fa7677b4522","total_cost_usd":0.8830755,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":65991,"cache_read_input_tokens":16967,"output_tokens":6719,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":65991,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":6719,"cache_read_input_tokens":16967,"cache_creation_input_tokens":65991,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":65991},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"modelUsage":{"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001":{"inputTokens":46592,"outputTokens":21,"cacheReadInputTokens":0,"cacheCreationInputTokens":0,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.046697,"contextWindow":200000,"maxOutputTokens":32000,"canonicalModel":"claude-haiku-4-5","provider":"firstParty"},"claude-opus-5":{"inputTokens":2,"outputTokens":6719,"cacheReadInputTokens":16967,"cacheCreationInputTokens":65991,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.8363785,"contextWindow":1000000,"maxOutputTokens":64000,"canonicalModel":"claude-opus-5","provider":"firstParty"}},"permission_denials":[],"terminal_reason":"completed","fast_mode_state":"off","fast_mode_disabled_reason":"sdk_opt_in_required","subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"{\"agent\":\"playtester\",\"verdict\":\"targets_missed\",\"summary\":\"The flown config does not launch: only the bottom sample is reachable and it arrives with 1.4% of tank, the middle and top samples crash on ascent, so every descent number in this sweep describes a ship that cannot get to the altitude it is descending from.\",\"findings\":[{\"id\":\"ascent_fails_above_band_floor\",\"gdd_ref\":\"2.5\",\"claim\":\"The base ship reaches the floor of the band on a ballistic arc with 29.5% of tank left and a 97-second EVA window, and the shop's tank tiers extend that to orbit; the sweep's first question is whether the base ship's climb survives its own launch.\",\"measured\":\"Verification sweep, ascents: bottom sample reached by arc only, apex 72,880 m, fuel_remaining 5.72 kg of 420 (1.4% margin, not 29.5%), EVA window 118.9 s, orbit_reached false ('out of fuel before circularising'). Middle (115,000 m) and top (215,000 m) both reached=false, why='crashed on ascent', arc apex 84,977 m and 89,362 m — 30,000 m and 126,000 m short of their samples. The band runs to 280,000 m and the ship's best arc apex anywhere in the sweep is 89,362 m, so 68% of the value gradient and Armstrong's module at the ceiling are unreachable at every upgrade tier flown.\",\"severity\":\"blocking\",\"kind\":\"tuning\"},{\"id\":\"descent_table_flown_from_unreachable_altitudes\",\"gdd_ref\":\"2.2\",\"claim\":\"The params' four peaks — empty 144.7 / 86.2, full hold 203.7 / 125.5, module 268.8 / 196.5 — are the top-of-band figures the heat_capacity ladder (208/222/238) is built around, and the module's plunge infeasibility is the endgame's mechanic.\",\"measured\":\"Committed_descents at top, empty: plunge 148.9 against the params' 144.7; full hold: 162.0 against 203.7; module: 368.3 against 268.8 — the module's plunge is 37% hotter than claimed and its best skimmed descent is 231.3, above every shield tier (max 238 clears it by only 2.9%, and at bottom and middle the best skimmed peaks are 223.3 and 231.5 with survives=false at every skim count). So the module is infeasible on a plunge (design intent holds) but also fails or barely scrapes on a skim (design intent broken). Separately these entries are flown from 215,000 m, which the ascent sweep says the ship cannot reach — the descent grid and the ascent grid disagree about whether the same ship exists at the same altitude.\",\"severity\":\"blocking\",\"kind\":\"tuning\"},{\"id\":\"full_hold_mass_is_a_ninth_of_the_claim\",\"gdd_ref\":\"2.4\",\"claim\":\"base_slots 7 at the catalog's top-third mean of 106.1 kg gives 742.7 kg of cargo and a 1,392.7 kg full hold — 2.14x dry mass, 'roughly doubles the ship', reproducing the GDD's flown ~1,398 kg.\",\"measured\":\"Verification sweep, cargo: kg_per_slot 17.36, full_hold_kg 121.5, full_hold_mass_ratio 1.19. The simulator drew slot mass from the catalog the sweep actually reads, not from the top third the balancer priced against, so the full hold adds 19% of dry mass rather than 114%. Every downstream number moves with it: full-hold touchdown measures 3.27 m/s against a claimed 4.4, staged ballistic coefficient at full hold is 132.7*1.19 = ~158 kg/m2 rather than the 285 the Researcher checked against real capsules, and the (196.5, 203.7] window the heat ladder was fitted into does not exist in flight — full hold at top plunges at 162.0, 46 bar units under the base capacity. The greed decision has no measured cost: full hold lands soft (3.27 m/s) from every sample at every pass count.\",\"severity\":\"blocking\",\"kind\":\"tuning\"},{\"id\":\"parachute_prediction_contradicted\",\"gdd_ref\":\"2.3\",\"claim\":\"parachute_area_m2 480 at Cd 1.8 predicts descent_speed_full_hold_ms 4.4 — a stated canopy whose predicted speed the flight can contradict, with 12% of margin under the 5.0 m/s soft line, which is what makes Parachute a real purchase.\",\"measured\":\"Verification sweep, parachute: independent=true, measured_full_hold_ms 3.27 against claimed 4.4, delta 1.13 m/s (26% low), full_hold_mass_kg 771.5 in the parachute block against 121.5 cargo + 650 dry = 771.5 — consistent internally, so the disagreement is entirely the cargo-mass error above, not the canopy. spread_across_bands_ms is 0. At 3.27 m/s the base canopy already lands 35% under the soft line, so Parachute tier 1 (1,000) and tier 2 (2,350) buy nothing measurable at any load the sweep flew except the module.\",\"severity\":\"significant\",\"kind\":\"tuning\"},{\"id\":\"ablation_optimum_is_one_pass_confirmed\",\"gdd_ref\":\"2.2\",\"claim\":\"cost_curve minimises at k=0 at all three samples and optimal_skims is 0/0/0 — the GDD's withdrawn 2-4-pass goal, restated as a feasibility rule instead of a cost rule.\",\"measured\":\"Confirmed by flight, and the grid says it is a design property not a tuning miss. Every descent in the verification sweep has cheapest_pass_count 1: bottom empty 12.5% of plate at 1 pass against 26.9 / 51.5 / 94.4 / 169.5 at 2-5; top module 35.4% against 64.8 / 92.8 / 137.1. Exploration sweep: skim_benefit_saturates is satisfied by 0.889 of 5,184 configs and skimming_cools_the_entry by 0.883 — skimming works everywhere, but no world was found where extra passes are cheaper. Growth 1.75 prices feathering out as intended: 18 passes at bottom cost 252,788% of plate. The design's own resolution (Revision 3: multi-pass is a feasibility rule enforced by heat_capacity) is the right one and should not be reopened.\",\"severity\":\"minor\",\"kind\":\"design\"},{\"id\":\"unstaged_pass_is_a_dead_option\",\"gdd_ref\":\"2.2\",\"claim\":\"Braking passes may be flown staged or unstaged and it is a real choice: unstaged costs 3x heat but keeps the engine for periapsis control.\",\"measured\":\"Verification sweep, unstaged_braking: bottom shallow_pass_peak_heat 424, survivable=false against a max capacity of 238; middle 88, survivable=true. Only two of three samples were flown and the two disagree by 4.8x, which is not a gradient any player could read. Exploration sweep: unstaged_pass_survivable is the rarest target in the whole grid at 0.080 — 415 of 5,184 worlds — and none of the 12 top-scoring configs satisfies it (all score 7/9 with unstaged_pass_survivable false, unstaged_peak_heat 285-334). That satisfaction rate is a statement about the rule, not the numbers: the 3x multiplier on a hull with Cd 0.4 against a shield Cd 1.4 makes the unstaged pass 3x hotter while braking 3.5x less, so it is dominated by construction.\",\"severity\":\"significant\",\"kind\":\"design\"},{\"id\":\"fuel_margin_is_the_grid_s_scarcest_tunable\",\"gdd_ref\":\"4.4\",\"claim\":\"The tank's first tier turns a hop into a stay: base ship arcs to the floor with 29.5% left, tier 1 circularises with 10.9%.\",\"measured\":\"Exploration sweep target rates: fuel_margin_sane 0.164 (851 of 5,184), launch_survives_itself 0.426, full_hold_lands_soft 0.222, greed_costs_something 0.167. Every config in the top block reaches its margin the same way — fuel_fraction 0.5 with dry_mass 200 and radius 800 — i.e. the grid solves fuel margin by shrinking the world by 250x, not by tuning the ship. On the locked planet (radius 200,000, mu 3.6e11) the measured arc margin is 1.4%, so this is a tuning problem with a narrow but non-empty solution set: raise exhaust velocity and tank, not thrust. Note the top block's hottest_climb_peak_heat spans 41.7 to 120 across configs that differ only in TWR, so the ascent heat exemption is doing real work and launch_survives_itself is thrust-sensitive.\",\"severity\":\"significant\",\"kind\":\"tuning\"},{\"id\":\"difficulty_does_not_rise_with_altitude\",\"gdd_ref\":\"2.6\",\"claim\":\"Value scales with altitude 1.0 to 5.5 and reentry risk rises with it on its own — one gradient producing the difficulty curve.\",\"measured\":\"Measured backwards for a plunge and flat for a skim. Committed_descents, empty ship plunge: bottom 94.1, middle 116.7, top 148.9 — risk does rise, 58% across the band. But best_skimmed_peak_heat runs bottom 82.5, middle 85.5, top 83.9: skimming erases the altitude gradient entirely, so a player who flies one skim faces the same heat at 215,000 m as at 65,000 m while earning 5.5x the value. The plain ablation_by_pass_count table agrees and inverts: top empty 1-pass costs 11.8% of plate against bottom empty's 12.5%, i.e. the top of the band is measurably cheaper to leave. Exploration sweep: difficulty_rises_with_altitude is satisfied by 0.559 of the grid, so this is reachable, not structural.\",\"severity\":\"significant\",\"kind\":\"tuning\"},{\"id\":\"module_touchdown_fails_at_every_sample\",\"gdd_ref\":\"2.6\",\"claim\":\"A pristine delivery requires the maxed canopy and a clean flare: 1,200 m2 at 4,370 kg gives 4.93 m/s, under the 5.0 line by 1.4%.\",\"measured\":\"Every endgame-haul descent in the sweep lands at touchdown_ms 7.69 with soft_landing false, identically at bottom, middle and top and at every pass count from 1 to 18. That is 54% over the 5.0 m/s soft line and above the module's own degradation threshold on any reading, so the module degrades one state on touchdown in every flight the simulator flew — pristine is unreachable and the triumph ending is not currently deliverable. The invariance across pass count confirms it is a canopy-versus-mass result, not a trajectory result, and the sweep flew the base 480 m2 canopy rather than the tier-2 1,200 m2 the balancer sized for the finale.\",\"severity\":\"significant\",\"kind\":\"tuning\"},{\"id\":\"heat_index_and_skim_multipliers_disagree_with_flight\",\"gdd_ref\":\"2.2\",\"claim\":\"ablation.heat_index bottom 100 / middle 116.7 / top 144.7, and skim_heat_multiplier [1, 0.48, 0.45, 0.44].\",\"measured\":\"The index holds and the multipliers do not. Committed_descents empty plunge: 94.1 / 116.7 / 148.9 against 100 / 116.7 / 144.7 — middle is exact, bottom 5.9% low, top 2.9% high. skims.skim_heat_multiplier_measured: bottom [1, 0.702, 0.702, 0.702], middle [1, 0.524, 0.524, 0.524], top [1, 0.439, 0.439, 0.439]. Only the top sample matches the params' 0.44-0.48; the bottom is 46% weaker than claimed. The multiplier is altitude-dependent in flight and a scalar array in the params, and the params' array happens to be the top-of-band case, so the ablation cost_curve is optimistic at bottom and middle. Saturation after one skim is confirmed everywhere — the 2nd and 3rd skims measure identically to the 1st at all three samples and all three entry depths.\",\"severity\":\"minor\",\"kind\":\"tuning\"}],\"proposed_changes\":[{\"path\":\"flight.fuel_capacity_kg\",\"current\":420,\"proposed\":900,\"reason\":\"The measured arc reaches the band floor with 1.4% of tank and crashes on ascent at both higher samples; tank is the direct lever on apex and the grid's fuel_margin_sane target (0.164) is only reachable by raising propellant fraction, at the cost of a heavier launch stack and a lower liftoff TWR.\"},{\"path\":\"flight.fuel_burn_kgs\",\"current\":6.5,\"proposed\":4.6,\"reason\":\"Raises exhaust velocity from 4,385 to ~6,200 m/s at unchanged thrust, which buys apex without buying launch mass — the only way to reach the 215,000 m sample the descent grid already assumes; it costs the ship its punchy 2.68 TWR feel and lengthens the burn, so re-check climb peak heat after.\"},{\"path\":\"cargo.base_slots\",\"current\":7,\"proposed\":24,\"reason\":\"The simulator measures 17.36 kg per slot, so 7 slots is a 121.5 kg hold — 19% of dry mass against the design's 'roughly doubles'; 24 slots restores the ~1,390 kg full hold the heat ladder, the ballistic coefficient and the parachute were all fitted against, and it costs the shop's storage tiers their meaning unless the catalog's floor masses are raised instead.\"},{\"path\":\"landing.parachute_area_m2\",\"current\":480,\"proposed\":300,\"reason\":\"Measured full-hold touchdown is 3.27 m/s against a 5.0 m/s line — 35% of unused margin means the chute is a formality and no parachute tier buys anything; shrinking the canopy puts the soft landing back inside reach of a competent deploy, at the cost of punishing a late deploy harder.\"},{\"path\":\"reentry.heat_capacity\",\"current\":208,\"proposed\":170,\"reason\":\"Measured plunge peaks are 94.1/116.7/148.9 empty and 102.8/127.2/162.0 full hold, all far under 208, so the bar is never in danger for an ordinary haul; 170 keeps every ordinary load plunging (top full hold 162.0, 5% margin) while restoring the reentry anxiety the pillar asks for.\"},{\"path\":\"upgrades[5].value\",\"current\":222,\"proposed\":185,\"reason\":\"Shield tier 1 must stay ordered above the new base capacity and below the module's measured skimmed peak of 231.3, otherwise the finale's aerobrake requirement is bought away; costs the tier some of its felt jump.\"},{\"path\":\"upgrades[11].value\",\"current\":238,\"proposed\":240,\"reason\":\"The module's best measured skimmed descent is 231.3 at top and 231.5 at middle, so the maxed tier must clear ~232 for the finale to be flyable at all; 240 gives a 3.7% margin and stays 128 under the measured plunge peak of 368.3, preserving the rule that no tier buys the plunge back.\"},{\"path\":\"reentry.unstaged_heat_multiplier\",\"current\":3,\"proposed\":2,\"reason\":\"Unstaged passes measure 424 bar units at the bottom sample against a 238 ceiling and the grid satisfies unstaged_pass_survivable in only 8% of 5,184 worlds — the choice is dominated by construction; 2x makes it survivable-but-expensive so the steering-versus-heat trade actually exists, at the cost of softening the incentive to stage early.\"},{\"path\":\"ablation.skim_heat_multiplier\",\"current\":[1,0.48,0.45,0.44],\"proposed\":[1,0.55,0.53,0.52],\"reason\":\"Flight measures 0.702 at bottom, 0.524 at middle, 0.439 at top — the params carry the top-of-band case as if it were universal, so the cost curve is optimistic low in the band; a mid-band value is the smallest honest correction until the field is made altitude-dependent.\"},{\"path\":\"ablation.heat_index\",\"current\":{\"bottom\":100,\"middle\":116.7,\"top\":144.7},\"proposed\":{\"bottom\":94.1,\"middle\":116.7,\"top\":148.9},\"reason\":\"Replaces the two inferred endpoints with the flown empty-ship plunge peaks so the index stops being a claim the audit checks against itself; costs nothing but a wider measured spread across the band.\"}],\"confidence_notes\":[\"The verification sweep is internally inconsistent and that limits every descent finding: the ascent block says middle and top crashed on ascent and never reached 115,000 m or 215,000 m, while the descent and committed_descent blocks report full flights from those same altitudes. The descents were evidently spawned at altitude rather than flown there, so they are valid physics for a ship placed in that state and say nothing about whether the campaign can put a ship there. Treat the ascent failure as the primary finding and the descent numbers as conditional on fixing it.\",\"The full-hold mass disagreement (121.5 kg measured against 1,392.7 kg claimed) is the single fault behind at least four findings — parachute speed, ballistic coefficient, the (196.5, 203.7] heat window, and greed-costs-something. I have proposed moving base_slots because that is the parameter in game_params.json, but the balancer's own catalog_concerns say the real fault may be the debris catalog's bottom-third masses (5.5-15.5 kg) or a live size_classes slot table. Whoever fixes it should decide which of the two is wrong before moving base_slots; moving both would overshoot by the crush factor.\",\"The exploration grid's top configurations are all radius 800 m with 22.4 m scale height — a world 250x smaller than the locked planet. Their satisfaction rates are still meaningful as statements about which targets are reachable anywhere, but no top config is a proposal, and I did not read their absolute values as transferable to planet.lock.json. In particular unstaged_pass_survivable at 0.080 is a strong structural signal precisely because it fails even in the worlds that solve everything else.\",\"Ballistic coefficient is the quantity the charter names as controlling whether aerobraking exists, and the sweep reports staged 132.7 and unstaged 464.3 kg/m2 for the empty ship — both comfortably above the 50 kg/m2 floor, so skimming is not at risk from this direction at any load. If base_slots is raised to 24 as proposed, full-hold staged beta rises to ~285 kg/m2, which is where the Researcher intended it and inside the real-capsule range; that change does not endanger the mechanic.\",\"The 7.69 m/s module touchdown is invariant across all three samples and all pass counts from 1 to 18, and equals the value the sweep reports for a 3,600 kg load on the base 480 m2 canopy. I read that as the simulator flying the base canopy for every load rather than applying the upgrade path, so the finale's pristine feasibility on the tier-2 1,200 m2 canopy was never actually flown — the balancer's 4.93 m/s prediction is still unmeasured, and that is the single highest-value cell to add to the next sweep.\",\"The simulator is a point mass with a perfect pilot. The 118.9-second EVA window is time above the band floor, not time a human can fly out, latch and stow; §4.5 already flags 97 s as an unanswered judgement and 118.9 s does not answer it. Nothing here measures tether handling, attitude error, debris collisions, the legibility of the commit floor in the cockpit, or whether any of this is tense rather than tedious.\"]}","ttft_ms":95857,"ttft_stream_ms":3444,"time_to_request_ms":326,"type":"result","duration_ms":95878,"uuid":"220fe195-cc7a-4f07-a892-e0ad89f476b6"}
